WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.789 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in a kurdish run jail in northern syria  the young muslim convert dubbed jihadi  jack  arrives to give his first interview  since his parents back home in oxford  were convicted of funding terrorism  by sending him money two  60 year old atheists being convicted for  islamic terrorism doesn't really make  much sense  i feel guilty for what i put them  through really that being said they  didn't actually do anything wrong they  sent me money to  leave etc and they didn't actually it's  not like they were actually funding some  sort of terrorist  activities jack letts was only a  schoolboy when he left home in may 2014  saying he wanted to learn arabic in  jordan  but four months later to his mother's  horror  he called home to say he was in syria i  wanted to fight the regime  and i never fought anyone except for the  regime but he says  he became disillusioned i left at a time  when  isis wasn't actually falling in on  itself i left because i opposed them i  genuinely opposed them i was played in  prison three times by isis  and then escaped from them if that makes  sense being against the regime i think  is a good thing but i definitely was  with the wrong people and that's  something i learned a long time ago now  he wants to come home  i know it doesn't it seems a bit  far-fetched at the moment but i'd love  to go home  britain's my home of course i'd want to  go home the british people like i said  they're my people but  i'm not sure they'd agree with that but  i have no intention of blowing them up  that's probably important  but this is a man who in an interview  last year said he would have been  prepared to detonate a suicide car bomb  when living in the former islamic state  stronghold of raqqa  attempts were made to de-radicalize jack  in 2015 and 16  but government funding was withdrawn  people like jack  and shamima begum there will always be a  threat  until you know one can assess them you  know one can work with them  i'm not one to say you know let's work  them with open arms i'd say let's put  them through due process  let's assess the risk this you know  protect the public and  and our country from any kind of risk  that these people would pose but also  look at them as an opportunity  to rehabilitate reintegrate but also use  them as  as tools to prevent others from falling  foul  to a narrative like isis our questions  were put to jack by a local journalist  the 23 year old has already been in  prison for two and a half years  and has no idea if his iraqi wife is  still alive  nor has he met his son i saw him in a  dream once  i have no idea what it looks like to be  honest and that's obviously hard to deal  with  that's sort of my life goal at the  moment  see my wife and child wherever it is  but he says no one's prepared to decide  what to do with him  and the home office won't comment on  individual cases  jack's parents received a 15-month  suspended sentence on friday  his mother sally lane told sky news on  the phone that she was incredibly  reassured to hear her son was well  it's been more than two years since they  spoke i really want to see them any more  than i've ever wanted to see them in my  whole life and  being more than two years not being able  to hear my like i said my mother's voice  is sort of a big deal for us  we were very close family and it's my  mum was my best friend now his mother  says the campaign will begin  to get him home the question is whether  he'd be welcome  emma birchley sky news

